  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nLooking at increasing northeast winds today with sfc high pressure \ncentered over the Great Lakes. Even with the cold front just south \nof the area this afternoon, the cooler and drier air is lagging \nbehind a bit. Therefore a Heat Advisory is in effect till 8 PM for \nareas south of a line from Greenville, MO to Murray, KY for one more \nday of heat indices over 100 degrees. Tomorrow (Labor Day) still \nlooks like the best day with highs right around 90 and dew points in \nthe mid-upper 50s. However, the upper ridge to the west looks to \nshift and build back over the region and brings us a warming trend \nfor Tuesday and Wednesday unfortunately.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday look to be uncomfortably hot again with highs \nin the mid to upper 90s Tuesday, and highs in the upper 90s to 100 \nin a few places Wednesday. Dew points back in the mid-upper 60s are \nexpected both days. This puts heat indices back into the 100-105 \ndegree range on Wednesday afternoon. The ridge breaks down to weaker \nzonal flow by Wednesday morning and allows a cold front to swing \nthrough Wednesday night into Thursday. NBM PoPs have increased in \nthe north this run to 70% up north and closer to 30% down south \nWednesday night. Thursday morning most of the Quad State is under 50-\n70% PoPs with chances diminishing in the afternoon and evening. QPF \nhas also increased across the north with now around 0.75\" up to \n1.25\" (this high end being more localized) from Wednesday night \nthrough Thursday. \n\nThe weak zonal flow aloft continues through the weekend allowing for \nsome messy 15-25% PoPs each day. However, temperatures will be more \npleasant and hover right around or just above normal.",
